WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.535
-We're back with
Jeremy Allen White, everybody.

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.003
Last time you were here,
we were talking backstage.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.739
It was right after the first
season of "The Bear" had aired.

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:08.908
I think people who watched it
were huge fans,

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.944
but certainly way more people
have watched it now.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:14.747
It must have been so exciting to
watch that wave of recognition.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:17.216
-It was.
I mean, it was amazing to have

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:19.285
that many people kind of connect
with the first season.

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:22.088
But then I think -- yeah,
like, the first season,

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:25.758
we made in this bubble with,
like, no real expectations.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:27.494
Nobody really knew
it was coming.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.196
And then, yeah, preparing for
the second season was, uh --

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.464
was a little bit different.
-Of course.

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:32.898
Well, then you have
the burden of pressure.

00:00:32.898 --> 00:00:34.633
People have these expectations.
I was so impressed

00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.603
because I felt like it was --
and I loved the first season --

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:38.905
I felt like it was
an improvement.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.239
And one of the reasons
it was an improvement

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.108
was there were huge risks.

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:43.610
It felt like there was
a tonal change,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.779
and you guys could have
skated on what you had.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.347
Were you all aware
as you started?

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:49.482
"Oh, look at this.
We're gonna actually

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:52.252
try to do something
a little bit more"?

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:53.920
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, I think you're right.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.554
It was absolutely a gamble.

00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:58.090
Kind of like doing
that tempo change, um,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.859
and broadening the world
a little bit.

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:02.962
But, um -- But it worked.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.865
And I think there's,
like, so much value in --

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.133
There were these great episodes.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.469
Like, my favorite episode
of the season was "Forks,"

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.071
which was pretty much just Ebon,
who plays Richie on the show.

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:15.475
And I loved that one. Yes?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:19.979
-I will say -- and I know
a lot has been said about it --

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.081
a lot has been written
about it, rightfully so --

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.716
the Christmas episode...
-Yeah.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.318
-...I think, as we come --
there you go.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:26.519
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.387
I think there should be
an annual tradition.

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:29.689
I think my family is
going to start doing it.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.590
We're going to watch it
Christmas morning.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.426
-Yeah.
-Just so we know

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.494
we're not that bad.
-Sure.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:36.696
-You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:39.098
-It's a real perspective-giver,
I think.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.266
Watch it early in the day.
No matter how bad it gets,

00:01:41.266 --> 00:01:43.469
it's like, "Remember,
at least we're not that bad."

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.138
-You can give yourselves
that gift.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:47.239
-Yeah, I think that's
a good gift to give each other.

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:49.742
So, it's based on
this restaurant, Mr. Beef.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.444
-Yeah.
-And the show creator,

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.080
he actually -- is that true
that he knew,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.714
uh, the guy
who runs that restaurant?

00:01:55.714 --> 00:01:57.917
-Yeah, Chris. It's two Chrises.
-Okay, great.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.285
-The creator of the show
is Chris,

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.621
and the owner of the restaurant
is Chris.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.122
Um, and, yeah,

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:04.523
they were in, like,
kindergarten together.

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:08.995
So, Chris Storer, our Chris,
who started the show, um,

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.497
he spent a lot of time
in that restaurant, growing up,

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:15.802
and, got to know it well.
And obviously, you know,

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.703
was a big inspiration
for making the show.

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:19.772
-Has the clientele changed
at Mr. Beef

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.441
since the show started?
-Yeah.

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:23.142
I mean, it's, like --
it's an old-school spot.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:27.413
And I think, you know, I don't
think they've lost that crowd.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.081
-Right.
-But, yeah,

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:31.617
now there's all sorts
of people that coming in.

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:33.086
that are fans of the show.

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:35.321
And I mean,
that's just, like, uh --

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.024
it's just such a nice thing
the show could do now.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.460
You know, it's, like,
in the second season,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.127
we visited
all these restaurants,

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:44.363
and now you have people
going to those restaurants.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.766
-That's really cool.
-It's really nice.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.134
-I bet that original group
of people that went to Mr. Beef

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:51.437
never even thought you could
Instagram a photo.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.739
-No, exactly. Yes, right.
-They were like, "Do you know,

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.541
this whole time,
we coulda put this online?"

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.143
-Yes. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.145
-"All these likes,
we let go for nothin'."

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.748
Um, I've been working
on my Chicago accent.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.549
-It's good. Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:04.883
-I'm very excited
to have you here,

00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:07.186
because this is clickbait.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:10.422
-It is clickbait.
-And I see it too often.

00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:13.359
I don't know why the algorithm
is, like, you were like...

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.461
So, it's a thing that says,
"These grandchildren look

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.430
exactly like
their celebrity grandparents."

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.432
And here's the photo.
-You guys know.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.567 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.369 line:0%
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Now, here's the thing.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.906 line:0%
That's you -- Uh,
that's you and Gene Wilder.

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.075 line:0%
-Gene Wilder, who's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.844 line:0%
And yet, even
if you click through --

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.946 line:0%
Have you ever clicked on it?
-Well, no,

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.749
because I know
that that's not my grandfather.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.818
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.986
-I had no reason
to give that click.

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:43.756
-Give away a free click.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.591
-But it's so weird.
Even if you click through,

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:49.028
you don't ever come up.
-Yeah.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.196
They're just baiting people.
-And I love --

00:03:51.196 --> 00:03:53.299
because I will say
it's really good bait.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:54.800
-Yeah.
-Because when you look at it,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.569 line:0%
you look at it
and your first thought is,

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.271 line:0%
"Oh, how come I didn't
figure that out on my own?"

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:00.439 line:0%
-You're kind of like,
"This is gonna be good."

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:02.374
-"I bet the other ones
are gonna be equally as good."

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.377
None of them are good.
-No. No, no, no.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.578
-But have you -- have you
been hip to this?

00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:08.647
Have people told it to you?
-For some -- Yeah.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:10.682
So, like, during the first
season of the show,

00:04:10.682 --> 00:04:14.620
I think people started talking
about the resemblance

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.858
that we bear, and it's --
I mean, it's true.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:21.727
And I love him so much.
Like, the first film

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.363
I think I watched
over and over again,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.464
as a kid, was
"Young Frankenstein."

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:28.100
And then I found
all the rest after.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.836
But, yeah.
-That is -- It's really funny

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.637
that, I guess, there's,
like, a clickbait farm,

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:34.773
like, office, where a guy, like,
runs into his editor

00:04:34.773 --> 00:04:36.408
and he's like,
"I think I got something!"

00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:38.544
-And it's been months.
-It's been months!

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:41.247
Nobody nobody's gonna get tired
of finding out this isn't true.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.082
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.117
-Congratulations on everything.
The new film is great.

00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:47.653
I'm so excited that everybody
has discovered "The Bear."

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.354
It is such a wonderful show.
And, you guys,

00:04:49.354 --> 00:04:51.090
that's Jeremy Allen White.
-Thank you so much.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.092
"The Iron Claw,"
theaters, December 22nd.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:55.094
We'll be right back
with Hannah Waddingham.

